: 0.59}, "rating_margin": 2.27, "sigma_race": 5.3, "sigma_total": 5.94, "baseline_margin": 1.3, "analyst_adjustment": 1.1, "key_factors": ["Peltola holds a narrow 1-7 point lead across recent polls, including a high-quality NYT/Siena survey", "Raters split between Tossup and Lean D, with none favoring Sullivan", "Peltola's fundraising edge: $37M raised versus Sullivan's $11.5M", "DOJ lawsuit over certification adds post-election uncertainty"], "rationale": "Recent surveys consistently show Peltola slightly ahead, raters tilt her way, and she holds a large fundraising advantage, while Kalshi prices her near 75 percent. Sullivan's incumbency in a Republican-leaning state keeps the margin narrow, so this lands as a modest Democratic edge rather than a clear favorite.", "watch": "The Justice Department's certification lawsuit, turnout among Alaska Native voters, or a late break toward Sullivan could shift the result.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Dan Sullivan (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Alaska's Senate race pits incumbent Republican Dan Sullivan against Democrat Mary Peltola, and it sits at the center of the battle for Senate control. Recent polls give Peltola a narrow edge, generally between one and seven points, with a high-quality New York Times/Siena survey showing her up seven. Raters split between tossup and lean-Democratic, and none place Sullivan ahead. Peltola has raised roughly three times as much money as Sullivan, though outside groups have spent somewhat more to boost the incumbent.\n\nThe state's Republican lean and Sullivan's incumbency keep the race close, and some Republican-leaning pollsters show him ahead. Kalshi markets price Peltola as a clear favorite. An unusual Justice Department lawsuit seeking to block certification if Sullivan loses adds uncertainty to both the count and the aftermath, and both candidates are competing hard for Alaska Native voters.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.5747, "margin": 0.87}, {"